/-!
# Off-diagonal evaluation kernel for the sequence algebra

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 088I (lemma-infinite-number), proof of the kernel assertion,
for a constant family of fields (blueprint: lem-sfb-sequence-tensor-off-diagonal-kernel).

The sequence algebra is the ordinary function algebra `ℕ+ → K`, with diagonal scalars.
Its tensor square carries Mathlib's multiplication and unit on the module tensor product.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace Algebra.TensorProduct

/-- The canonical off-diagonal coordinate evaluation on the tensor square of the sequence
algebra: a pure tensor `a ⊗ b` has value `a n * b m` at the ordered pair `(n,m)`.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 088I (lemma-infinite-number), displayed map in its proof,
specialized to a constant family of fields
(blueprint: lem-sfb-sequence-tensor-off-diagonal-kernel). -/
def sequenceOffDiagonalEvaluation (K : Type u) [Field K] :
    ((ℕ+ → K) ⊗[K] (ℕ+ → K)) →ₐ[K] ({p : ℕ+ × ℕ+ // p.1 ≠ p.2} → K) :=
  AlgHom.pi fun p ↦ Algebra.TensorProduct.productMap
    (Pi.evalAlgHom K (fun _ : ℕ+ ↦ K) p.val.1)
    (Pi.evalAlgHom K (fun _ : ℕ+ ↦ K) p.val.2)

/-- The kernel of off-diagonal evaluation on the tensor square of the sequence algebra is
exactly the ideal generated by the diagonal coordinate tensors.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 088I (lemma-infinite-number), proof, kernel assertion for a
constant family of fields (blueprint: lem-sfb-sequence-tensor-off-diagonal-kernel).

Proof: Write `t` in the kernel as a finite sum `∑ i, uᵢ ⊗ vᵢ`. The span `U` of the `uᵢ`
is finite-dimensional. Apply `Submodule.exists_finset_eval_injective`
(lem-weil-finite-coordinate-detection) to obtain a finite coordinate set `S` detecting `U`.
For each `m`, the column `cₘ = ∑ i, vᵢ(m) • uᵢ` belongs to `U`. If `m ∉ S`, its values
on `S` vanish by off-diagonal evaluation, hence `cₘ = 0`. If `m ∈ S`, off-diagonal
vanishing gives `cₘ = cₘ(m) • eₘ`. Subtract the finite sum
`d = ∑ m ∈ S, cₘ(m) • (eₘ ⊗ eₘ)`; every column of `w = t - d` is zero.
Flip `w`. For every evaluation `fₘ`, balancing identifies `(fₘ ⊗ id)(flip w)` with
`1 ⊗ cₘ(w) = 0`. The evaluations jointly detect zero, so
`TensorProduct.eq_zero_of_forall_rTensor_eq_zero`
(lem-sfb-vector-space-tensor-family-zero-detection) gives `flip w = 0`, hence `w = 0`.
Thus `t = d` lies in the generated ideal. Conversely, each diagonal coordinate tensor
has zero off-diagonal evaluation; the kernel is an ideal, giving the reverse inclusion. -/
theorem ker_sequenceOffDiagonalEvaluation (K : Type u) [Field K] :
    RingHom.ker (sequenceOffDiagonalEvaluation K).toRingHom =
      Ideal.span (Set.range fun n : ℕ+ ↦
        (Pi.single n (1 : K)) ⊗ₜ[K] (Pi.single n (1 : K))) := by
  sorry

end Algebra.TensorProduct
